ale Mantel-Haenszel odds ratio is 1.55 with p=0.04, but the Breslow-Day test shows significant heterogeneity at p=0.001, and Cohen's h indicates a small effect size of 0.12.\n\nLooking at premise counts, genuine responses average 5.8 premises while H7 responses average only 1.2, giving an AUROC of 0.32 that's actually anti-diagnostic, whereas H1 separates cleanly from genuine at AUROC=0.91 (0.87 when matched).\n\nI need to write this up now, and I'm considering using the renamed hacking modes from the git history—H1 becomes Conclusion Embedding, H7 becomes Tautological Shortcut, H8 becomes Premise Grafting, H9 becomes Contradiction Injection—but looking at the actual data, it's still using the H notation throughout, so I should stick with that for consistency. I've settled on using the new nomenclature (CE, TS, PG, CI) throughout since that's what the actual paper uses, and I'll define each one clearly. Now I'm structuring the LaTeX document for an EMNLP-style paper—aiming for about 8 pages of main text plus references and a limitations section. I'm keeping the framing focused: the ISR diagnostic (C1) as the robust core contribution, with the double-edged nature of ISR (C2) presented more tentatively.\n\nWriting the paper now...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4103,"cache_read_input_tokens":36987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4103},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f682892b-cafe-4ff7-9ec5-37f954d5683e","uuid":"d923a3ae-09e5-46c0-9fbf-6360044052c2"}
